WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
This is The Spread, your energy
markets briefing for Friday,

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.440
the twenty-third of October.

3
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:10.240
This episode is written with AI,
and read by an AI clone of my voice.

4
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.000
Oil jumped about four percent on
Thursday after deadly attacks in Saudi

5
00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:18.640
Arabia, and Iran now claims seven
more vessels were hit in the Gulf.

6
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.107
Freight has become the real
constraint on both crude and diesel,

7
00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:24.840
and it has shut US diesel out of Europe.

8
00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:28.640
On the markets. December Brent traded
at one hundred and eleven dollars

9
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.320
seventy, according to OilPrice dot com.

10
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.800
That was a gain of two dollars ninety,
after a high of one hundred and

11
00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:38.880
twelve dollars seventy. Rigzone had
Brent above one hundred and thirteen

12
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:42.040
dollars, most likely at a
different point in the day.

13
00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.080
European gas storage was seventy-four
point six percent full on the twenty-first

14
00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:50.400
of October, barely changed, according
to Gas Infrastructure Europe.

15
00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.360
Starting in Saudi Arabia.

16
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.573
Rigzone ties the rally to the
deadly attacks on the kingdom,

17
00:00:55.627 --> 00:00:58.600
renewed tension with
Iran, and tight diesel.

18
00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.000
Saudi Aramco evacuated its headquarters
as Houthi attacks continue.

19
00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.280
Aramco's chief executive, Amin Nasser,
said Riyadh is in talks with foreign

20
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.440
governments about access
to their strategic storage.

21
00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:14.347
The idea is to keep Aramco's customers
supplied through disruptions.

22
00:01:14.373 --> 00:01:17.120
He named no partners and no volumes.

23
00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:21.280
Citi has shifted too. Three months
ago it was forecasting Brent at sixty

24
00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.560
to sixty-five dollars by year-end.

25
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.800
Now it sees more upside risk.

26
00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.613
Its argument is that Gulf
crude is still getting out,

27
00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:32.080
but not cheaply, with more than one
million barrels a day moving between

28
00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.560
vessels in ship-to-ship transfers.

29
00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:38.693
That bypass route is exactly what
Iran now says it is going after.

30
00:01:38.747 --> 00:01:40.747
Which brings us to the Gulf itself.

31
00:01:40.773 --> 00:01:44.640
The navy of Iran's Islamic Revolutionary
Guard Corps says seven vessels

32
00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:48.240
were attacked or struck mines across
the Gulf and the Strait of Hormuz

33
00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:52.400
in twenty-four hours. It says four of
them were hit together while transferring

34
00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.720
oil, on a route Tehran calls unauthorised.

35
00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:57.733
This is Iran's claim alone.

36
00:01:57.867 --> 00:02:00.533
President Trump has accused
Tehran of faking hits,

37
00:02:00.587 --> 00:02:04.720
and no owner, flag state or navy
has confirmed any of the seven.

38
00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.920
If the strikes are real, they would
push up war-risk cover for tanker

39
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.880
owners and tie up more ships in
a market that is already tight.

40
00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.467
Turning to diesel. In a note
dated the eighteenth of October,

41
00:02:15.493 --> 00:02:19.120
Kpler said medium-range tanker
freight rose about thirty percent week

42
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.040
on week. That pushed the arbitrage
for shipping diesel from the US Gulf

43
00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:27.440
Coast to Rotterdam to minus
twenty-two dollars thirty a tonne.

44
00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:31.200
The same freight move shut Indian
diesel out of the Mediterranean.

45
00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:35.760
So Europe's two main swing suppliers
are priced out at the same time.

46
00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:38.640
And US refiners are already
running near their limit,

47
00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.280
according to the EIA. Utilisation
averaged more than ninety-five percent

48
00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:45.080
from May through September.

49
00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.107
That's the highest for those
months since nineteen ninety-eight,

50
00:02:48.133 --> 00:02:50.040
driven by diesel margins.

51
00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:53.680
US distillate stocks rose about one
point seven million barrels in the

52
00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.840
week to the sixteenth of October.

53
00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.720
That took them to roughly one hundred
and four point three million barrels.

54
00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.040
One possible knock-on is that barrels
which can't reach Europe build

55
00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:06.840
up in US tanks, while
European diesel cracks firm.

56
00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:12.000
Over to gas. QatarEnergy has signed
two long-term deals to buy US liquefied

57
00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:15.093
natural gas from Cheniere,
gCaptain reported.

58
00:03:15.147 --> 00:03:17.400
They cover up to four
million tonnes a year,

59
00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.707
running through to twenty fifty-four.

60
00:03:19.733 --> 00:03:23.280
It's a Gulf exporter protecting its
own customer book while Hormuz holds

61
00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:24.507
it back.

62
00:03:24.613 --> 00:03:29.480
Splash twenty-four seven describes
Gulf LNG exports as near-paralysed.

63
00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:34.000
Cargoes are being redirected and Asian
buyers are turning to other suppliers.

64
00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.120
Gas prices are approaching thirty
dollars per million British thermal

65
00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:37.960
units.

66
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.520
And in China, the buffers are thinning.

67
00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.280
The country's petrochemical glut,
built over years of overproduction,

68
00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.640
has largely been used up as
the war chokes feedstock flows,

69
00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:49.080
according to Rigzone.

70
00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:53.440
On crude, OilPrice dot com reports
that China drew on its one point three

71
00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:56.480
billion barrels of stocks
in most months after March,

72
00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:00.840
while cutting imports. Rising imports
now mean it needs to draw less.

73
00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.880
That draw-down was one of
the market's main cushions.

74
00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:07.520
If China is buying rather than
drawing, it competes with everyone else

75
00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:11.720
for scarce seaborne crude, though
the reports give no import volumes.

76
00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.000
A few more things.

77
00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:16.800
Moscow said the first Russian diesel
cargo bound for the US would sail

78
00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:20.320
on the twenty-second of October,
but none of today's reports confirm

79
00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:21.400
a departure.

80
00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:24.200
And offshore shut-in figures
for Hurricane Isaias,

81
00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:27.320
from the US Bureau of Safety
and Environmental Enforcement,

82
00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.000
are still unpublished.

83
00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.800
What to watch next. Daily European
storage readings run through to the

84
00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.320
twenty-eighth. The EIA's weekly numbers,
covering the week to the twenty-third,

85
00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.400
are due on Wednesday the twenty-eighth.

86
00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:42.160
And look for the overdue
Isaias shut-in data,

87
00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:45.920
which was due on the sixteenth, and
for any sign that the Russian cargo

88
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:47.200
has actually sailed.

89
00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:51.520
That's the brief. Sources for everything
you heard are in the show notes.
